<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Evaluation of FedSPC</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate FedSPC combined with PFL methods under class-distribution heterogeneity. The FL setup uses <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=10</annotation></semantics></math> clients, <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=100</annotation></semantics></math> communication rounds, and full client participation. Experiments are conducted on CIFAR-100 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky et al. (2009)</a></cite> using ViT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al. (2020)</a></cite>, ResNet-34 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. (2016)</a></cite>, and VGG-11 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Simonyan and Zisserman (2014)</a></cite>. To assess robustness across datasets, we also evaluate ResNet-34 on Tiny-ImageNet-200 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russakovsky et al. (2015)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All methods are implemented in PyTorch within a unified FL framework, where methods differ only in their local update rules, and experiments are run on Ubuntu 22.04 with an NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU. All models use a batch size of 64. Local epochs are selected from <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\{1,2,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\{1,2,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=5</annotation></semantics></math> used in our experiments. Learning rates are selected from <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\{0.005,0.01,0.02\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.005</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\{0.005,0.01,0.02\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.02</annotation></semantics></math> used for ResNet-34 and VGG-11 and <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.005</annotation></semantics></math> used for ViT. For FedProx and Ditto, regularization parameters are tuned over <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0.001,0.01,0.1,1.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.001</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0.001,0.01,0.1,1.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> for FedProx and <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> for Ditto used across all settings. The ViT is trained from scratch with patch size 4, 12 transformer layers, and 12 attention heads.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use centralized training on the union of all client data as a pooled-data reference. The centralized model is trained for 200 epochs with learning rate 0.1 and achieves Top-1 accuracies of 68.20%, 79.57%, and 71.58% on CIFAR-100 with ViT, ResNet-34, and VGG-11, respectively, and 67.95% on Tiny-ImageNet-200 with ResNet-34.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Class-distribution heterogeneity is generated using Dirichlet partitioning with <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{0.01,0.1,1.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{0.01,0.1,1.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where smaller <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> indicates stronger class-distribution skew. For each class, client assignment proportions are sampled from a Dirichlet distribution, and samples are assigned accordingly. Figures <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5 Evaluation of FedSPC ‣ FedSPC: Shared Parameter Correction for Personalized Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5 Evaluation of FedSPC ‣ FedSPC: Shared Parameter Correction for Personalized Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show the resulting client-level distributions for CIFAR-100 and Tiny-ImageNet-200 respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13748v1/CIFAR100.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/118;" width="381" height="118" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
<span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dirichlet data partitioning on CIFAR-100.</span>
The x-axis denotes clients and the y-axis denotes classes. Each panel shows the class distribution across clients under a different Dirichlet parameter: <math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> on the left, <math id="S5.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> in the middle, and <math id="S5.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> on the right.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13748v1/Tiny200.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/118;" width="381" height="118" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
<span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dirichlet data partitioning on Tiny-ImageNet.</span>
The x-axis denotes clients and the y-axis denotes classes. Each panel shows the class distribution across clients under a different Dirichlet parameter: <math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> on the left, <math id="S5.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> in the middle, and <math id="S5.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> on the right.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>PFL Methods and Parameter Partitioning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare each PFL method with its corresponding FedSPC variant. FedSPC is applied only to the shared part, while the personalized part remains local and is not corrected. The selected baselines cover the three parameter-sharing settings considered in this work. FedPer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arivazhagan et al. (2019)</a></cite>, FedRep <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Collins et al. (2021)</a></cite>, and FedBABU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al. (2021)</a></cite> use a shared feature extractor and a personalized classifier. LG-FedAvg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. (2020)</a></cite> uses a personalized feature extractor and a shared classifier. Ditto <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2021b)</a></cite> maintains a global model together with a personalized local model through proximal regularization. Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 PFL Methods and Parameter Partitioning ‣ 5 Evaluation of FedSPC ‣ FedSPC: Shared Parameter Correction for Personalized Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarizes the partitioning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Parameter partitioning across PFL methods.</figcaption>
